\f1\b\fs48 The Curious Case of Clinton Confusion\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::1>
\f2 In the annals of cosmic misunderstandings, few have had consequences as disproportionately absurd as the Great Clinton Confusion of 2023. It began, as these things often do, with a simple case of mistaken identity at the Sublime Point Christmas party, where a visiting evolutionary biologist was confused with the disgraced former president of a vehicle manufacturing corporation. Such confusion might seem unlikely, except that both men had achieved a certain notoriety in their respective fields for being, as The Johnny might describe it, "obstinately correct about everything."\
Clinton Richard Dawkins was, of course, the celebrated evolutionary biologist whose books on genetic selfishness had influenced generations of scientists. W. Jay Clinton, on the other hand, was the final president of the Arkansas Chuggabug Motor Company, makers of the infamous Grizzly\'97a vehicle which had the aerodynamic properties of a garden shed falling down a mountain and the fuel efficiency of a bonfire in a petroleum distillery.\
The Chuggabug Grizzly had once dominated the highways of Ulyssia much as the Gyges Disc would later dominate interdimensional barriers\'97with catastrophic enthusiasm and minimal regard for the laws of physics. Each successive model year saw the truck grow ever larger, as if the designers were compensating for some collective mechanical inadequacy. By its final year of production, the Grizzly SR-9000 "Apex Predator" Edition stood taller than most suburban homes and required a special license typically reserved for operators of industrial mining equipment.\
The hood ornament\'97a snarling brown bear cast in chromium-plated zinc alloy\'97had evolved with each model year much as the Elvi evolved after escaping the Layer. The earliest bears had been merely decorative, no larger than a man's fist. But competition from other manufacturers (particularly the Rogue Buffalo Company's "Stampede" truck with its charging bison emblem) triggered an evolutionary arms race of ornamental fauna. The final iteration of the Grizzly's bear stood nearly a meter tall and was hinged to rear up on its back legs when the horn was pressed, a feature that delighted owners and terrified pedestrians in equal measure.\
The truck's engine was a marvel of misplaced engineering effort. With sixteen cylinders arranged in a configuration that Eloise Shakti once described as "like watching a drunken spider try to knit," it consumed fuel with the same desperate enthusiasm that Agent Jones fought against his gospel music infection. The Grizzly's official fuel consumption rating was listed as "Yes," and owners were advised to establish personal relationships with petroleum refineries.\
Its braking system operated on the principle of suggestion rather than hydraulic certainty. When Grizzly owners applied the brakes, the vehicle considered this input as merely one option among many, much like CEO Dunhilton considered democratic process\'97a quaint formality to be acknowledged and subsequently ignored.\
The company's marketing slogan\'97"Be the Bear. Buy the Chuggabug."\'97spoke to consumers with the same persuasive authority that the Sages used when manipulating The Johnny toward dimensional catastrophe. Owners would proudly affix bumper stickers (to bumpers large enough to moonlight as airport runways) declaring "Grizzly Driver: Approaching Fast, Cornering Slow."\
This last point proved prophetically accurate. The SR-9000's final design flaw\'97that magnificent bear ornament\'97created what aerodynamics experts termed "catastrophic directional stubbornness." At speeds above sixty miles per hour, the ornament generated lift with the efficiency of an airplane wing, but only on whichever side of the truck was facing into the wind. This caused the vehicle to veer unpredictably, like Chip Stockwell attempting to navigate social situations at the Chuglinghams' barbecue.\
Turning corners became an exercise in probability theory. The Grizzly might follow the driver's intended path, or it might decide that continuing in a straight line through whatever buildings, vehicles, or geographical features lay ahead was the more appealing option. As the company's final internal memo grimly noted, "The bear on the hood has taken control of the herd."\
The customer fatality rate climbed with the same alarming trajectory as temporal anomalies at Sublime Point just before midnight on December 25th. Insurance companies began requiring Grizzly owners to prepay their premiums and prearrange their funerals. The motor vehicle department created a special designation on death certificates: "Ursine Navigation Error."\
Yet even this might not have spelled the company's doom had W. Jay Clinton not been photographed in a compromising position with the prototype ornament for the planned SR-10000 model\'97a bear so large it required its own VIN number. When confronted by shareholders, Clinton infamously declared, "I did not have sexual relations with that bear," a statement that contained approximately the same amount of truth as Fridge Force's public information about stasis technology.\
The resulting scandal forced the company into bankruptcy proceedings faster than The Johnny could take credit for someone else's research. The remaining Grizzlies were recalled and ceremonially crushed into cubes, though several escaped this fate and can still occasionally be spotted in rural areas, typically stuck in straight lines through forests or embedded in the sides of buildings.\
And so it was that when a Sublime Point secretary mistakenly introduced the visiting evolutionary biologist as "W. Jay Clinton, former president," several scientists dove for cover, one administrator called the vehicle recall center, and Eloise Shakti\'97never one to miss an opportunity\'97immediately offered him a position researching "alternative applications for bear ornaments in dimensional stabilization technology."\
The real Clinton Richard Dawkins, thoroughly confused but intrigued by the dimensional research possibilities, simply adjusted his glasses and remarked, "I suppose in some parallel universe, I might indeed have had inappropriate relations with automotive decorations. That's the wonder of infinite dimensional possibility."\
The Johnny, overhearing this, immediately claimed to have thought of it first.\
